an individual incapacitated to an extent that prevents participation in employment, education, or training;

2. a person in the home responsible for the full‑time care of an ill or incapacitated individual. Care must be provided on a substantially continuous basis under the following conditions:

a. the individual has been determined physically or mentally impaired by the Department; and

b. no other household member can provide the care; and

c. the individual must be in the home but need not be a member of the assistance unit;

3. a pregnant female in the second or third trimester of a confirmed pregnancy;

4. an individual who has a temporary illness or injury which prevents employment or training.

CONNECTICUT DEPARTMENT OF INCOME MAINTENANCEPRIVATE UNIFORM POLICY MANUAL _______________________________________________________________________________ Date: 7-1-89 Transmittal: UP-89-23 3510.20 _______________________________________________________________________________ Section: Type: Procedural Eligibility Requirement POLICY _______________________________________________________________________________ Chapter: Program: Employment and Training Requirements FS _______________________________________________________________ Subject: Exemptions from Registration Requirements _______________________________________________________________________________

3510.20 All of the following members of a Food Stamp assistance unit are exempt from employment and training registration and other employment and training requirements:

A. an individual age 60 or over;

B. an individual under 16;

C. an individual age 16 or 17 who:

1. is not the designated provider in the household; (cross-reference 3510.50); or

2. attends school; or

3. is enrolled in an employment training program at least half-time.

D. an individual incapacitated to an extent that prevents participation in employment or training;

E. a caretaker responsible for the full-time care of one of the following:

1. a dependent child under the age of six. The caretaker loses this exemption at the first recertification after the child's 6th birthday; or

2. an incapacitated individual whose impairment requires continuous care;

F. eligible students who must meet specific technical eligibility employment and training requirements (cross reference: 3020) which take precedence over procedural employment and training requirements;

G. an individual employed or self-employed 30 hours per week or more, or who has earnings at least the equivalent of 30 hours per week x the Federal minimum wage;

H. participants in a drug or alcoholic treatment and rehabilitation program;

CONNECTICUT DEPARTMENT OF INCOME MAINTENANCE UNIFORM POLICY MANUAL _______________________________________________________________________________ Date: 7-1-89 Transmittal: UP-89-23 3510.20 page 2 _______________________________________________________________________________ Section: Type: Procedural Eligibility Requirement POLICY _______________________________________________________________________________ Chapter: Program: Employment and Training Requirements FS _______________________________________________________________ Subject: Exemptions from Registration Requirements _______________________________________________________________________________

3510.23 I. AFDC recipients who comply with AFDC employment and training requirements;

J. recipients of Unemployment Compensation benefits, including anyone with benefits pending, when the application process includes registering for work;

K. migrant workers who have a contract to work within 30 days but are not yet working;

L. SSI applicants, who apply for Food Stamps and SSI through the Social Security office joint application procedure, while determination of SSI eligibility is pending. Other SSI applicants are not exempt.

CONNECTICUT DEPARTMENT OF INCOME MAINTENANCEPRIVATE UNIFORM POLICY MANUAL _______________________________________________________________________________ Date: 7-1-89 Transmittal: UP-89-23 3510.35 _______________________________________________________________________________ Section: Type: Procedural Eligibility Requirement POLICY _______________________________________________________________________________ Chapter: Program: AFDC Employment and Training Requirements FS _______________________________________________________________ Subject: Good Cause for Non-Compliance _______________________________________________________________________________

3510.35 An individual for whom participation in the employment and training program is mandatory may claim good cause for non-compliance with a specific employment and training requirement after registration.

A. AFDC

1. Good cause is established by one or more of the following:

a. circumstances beyond the individual's control, such as, but not limited to:

(1) illness;

(2) illness of another household member;

(3) if necessary child care is not available and payment cannot be guaranteed by the Department;

(4) an emergency;

(5) the unavailability or inaccessibility of transportation;

b. conditions of employment, such as, but not limited to:

(1) discrimination on the basis of age, sex or color;

(2) working without being paid on schedule;

(3) requiring the caretaker relative of a child under 6 to participate in employment or training more than 20 hours per week;

(4) net loss of income as a result of accepting employment;

(5) unsuitability (cross-reference 3510.40).

2. If good cause is expected to last less than 60 days, the individual must resume participation as soon as practical after the reason no longer exists.

CONNECTICUT DEPARTMENT OF INCOME MAINTENANCE UNIFORM POLICY MANUAL _______________________________________________________________________________ Date: 9-21-96 Transmittal: UP-96-17 3510.35 page 2 _______________________________________________________________________________ Section: Type: Procedural Eligibility Requirement POLICY _______________________________________________________________________________ Chapter: Program: AFDC Employment and Training Requirements FS _______________________________________________________________ Subject: Good Cause for Non-Compliance _______________________________________________________________________________

3510.35 B. Food Stamps

1. Good cause is established by one or more of the following:

a. circumstances beyond the individual's control, such as, but not limited to:

(1) illness;

(2) illness of another household member;

(3) an emergency;

(4) the unavailability or inaccessibility of transportation;

b. conditions of employment, such as, but not limited to:

(1) discrimination on the basis of age, sex or color;

(2) working without being paid on schedule;

(3) unsuitability (cross-reference 3510.40 for what constitutes unsuitable employment).

2. If good cause is expected to last less than 60 days, the individual must resume participation as soon as practical after the reason no longer exists.

3. If good cause is expected to last 60 days or more, the individual is considered exempt (cross reference - Exemptions 3510.20, 3510.23).

CONNECTICUT DEPARTMENT OF INCOME MAINTENANCEPRIVATE UNIFORM POLICY MANUAL _______________________________________________________________________________ Date: 7-1-89 Transmittal: UP-89-23 3510.40 _______________________________________________________________________________ Section: Type: Procedural Eligibility Requirement POLICY _______________________________________________________________________________ Chapter: Program: AFDC Employment and Training Requirements FS _______________________________________________________________ Subject: Suitable Employment _______________________________________________________________________________

3510.40 Factors which determine the unsuitability of employment include some conditions which apply to the individual's circumstances and some which apply to the employment itself. A job is unsuitable when it can be described as follows:

A. it is not within the documented physical and mental capability of the individual;

B. it interferes with the individual's religious beliefs or observances;

C. the distance to the job is unreasonable in that:

1. it is neither within walking distance nor is there available public transportation; or

2. commuting time is more than two hours per day, not including trips to provide child care;

D. the wage is less than the higher of:

1. the Federal minimum wage; or

2. the State minimum wage;

E. the employer or potential employer is discriminatory;

F. the job is the site of a strike or lockout;

G. a condition of employment is either joining or not joining a legitimate labor organization;

H. the job poses an unreasonable risk to health or safety;

I. the job does not have a regular pay schedule;

J. employment offered within the first 30 days of participation is not in the individual's major field of experience.

CONNECTICUT DEPARTMENT OF SOCIAL SERVICESPRIVATE UNIFORM POLICY MANUAL _______________________________________________________________________________ Date: 11-01-06 Transmittal: UP-07-04 3512.10 _______________________________________________________________________________ Section: Type: Procedural Eligibility Requirements POLICY _______________________________________________________________________________ Chapter: Program: Work Requirements FS _______________________________________________________________ Subject: Exemptions _______________________________________________________________________________

3512.10 A. An individual is exempt from the work requirement if he or she is:

1. under age 18;

2. age 50 or older;

3. physically or mentally unfit for employment as verified by a medical professional or as observed by the eligibility worker;

4. a member of the same Food Stamp assistance unit as a child under age 18 regardless of whether the individual cares for the child or whether the child is eligible for Food Stamps;

5. pregnant;

6. otherwise exempt from Food Stamp employment and training registration requirements (Cross Reference 3510);

7. residing in an area exempted from the work requirement by a waiver approved by the Food and Nutrition Service due to high unemployment or the lack of available jobs for clients with certain vocational skills.

B. Those months during which a recipient is exempt from the work requirement are not counted toward the three month time limit.

